Live Transcribe for Linux
=========================

Hold the hotkey (Right Ctrl at first), speak, and let go: the text is typed into the app you're
using. The tray's Settings… changes the hotkey and the rest.

What the package installs
-------------------------

/usr/bin/livetranscribe
    The app. Started with no command (from the desktop's menu), it runs dictation; `livetranscribe
    --help` lists the rest.

/usr/lib/live-transcribe/openvino (in the AppImage, usr/share/live-transcribe/openvino)
    The OpenVINO 2026.2.1 runtime the app runs speech to text on: Intel's prebuilt libraries for
    Ubuntu 22.04, unmodified, each under the name its archive links to it (its soname). They are
    Intel's, under the Intel OpenVINO Distribution License in licenses/EULA.txt, which allows
    redistributing the files listed in licenses/redist.txt; TBB and the other components are under
    the licences in licenses/.

/usr/lib/live-transcribe/sherpa-onnx (in the AppImage, usr/lib)
    sherpa-onnx 1.13.8's C API and ONNX Runtime 1.28.2, which run the speech models other than
    Qwen3-ASR, on the CPU. sherpa-onnx is k2-fsa's, under the Apache License 2.0, and ONNX Runtime
    is Microsoft's, under the MIT licence, with its third-party notices: all three are in
    /usr/share/doc/live-transcribe/sherpa-onnx.

/usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70-live-transcribe-keyboard.rules (the deb and rpm)
    Lets whoever is logged in at the machine read the keyboards, which hold-to-talk needs: it
    watches the hotkey in /dev/input. Any program that user runs can then read what they type,
    as any X11 program always could. Removing the package removes the rule.
    The AppImage can't install it. To add it by hand:
        sudo install -m 644 <the rule> /etc/udev/rules.d/70-live-transcribe-keyboard.rules
        sudo udevadm control --reload-rules
        sudo udevadm trigger --subsystem-match=input --action=change
    (The AppImage carries the rule in usr/share/live-transcribe.)

The AppImage carries the app's libraries but, like other AppImages, uses the desktop's own for
graphics, fonts and sound (ALSA's libasound), and its CA certificates for the download below.
Every desktop has them; a bare container or server may not.

What the app downloads
----------------------

On first start, the speech model, Nerdstorm/Qwen3-ASR-0.6B-Sinhala-OpenVINO from Hugging Face
(about 1.1 GB, CC BY-SA 4.0), into ~/.local/share/live-transcribe/models, checking the connection
with the system's CA certificates (the deb and rpm depend on ca-certificates). Settings › Models
downloads the others when you choose one: NVIDIA's Parakeet TDT 0.6B v2 and v3 (0.5 GB each,
CC BY 4.0) and Cohere Transcribe (1.7 GB, Apache 2.0; it writes the one of its 14 languages chosen
there), which the sherpa-onnx project publishes on GitHub. Each file is checked against the size and SHA-256 the app was built with. It keeps its
settings in ~/.config/live-transcribe and OpenVINO's compiled models in ~/.cache/live-transcribe.

The NPU
-------

On an Intel Core Ultra the default speech model runs on the NPU when Intel's NPU driver is
installed: intel-npu-driver on Fedora, Arch and openSUSE, or Intel's packages from
https://github.com/intel/linux-npu-driver/releases on Ubuntu and Debian. Without it the model runs
on the CPU, more slowly. Parakeet and Cohere Transcribe always run on the CPU.

Desktops
--------

Dictation types into apps on COSMIC, and on Sway, Hyprland and other wlroots desktops. GNOME,
KDE Plasma and X11 desktops come in later versions; there the app starts, and its Settings window
says why dictation can't.

https://github.com/Nerdstorm/LiveTranscribe
